    content: `<p>Student protests have been a significant part of India's democratic fabric. From the freedom struggle to modern-day movements, students have always been at the forefront of social change. In 2026, several student movements have gained attention across India. Here is a guide for parents to understand these movements and how to discuss them with their children.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recent Student Movements in India (2026)</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="min-w-full bg-white border border-gray-200 shadow-sm rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm"><thead class="bg-[#035c00]"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-white text-left">Movement</th><th class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-white text-left">Key Demand</th><th class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-white text-left">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">NEET Paper Leak Protest</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b">Cancellation of exam, NTA reform</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b text-green-700 font-bold">Re-exam conducted</td></tr><tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">CBSE Fee Hike Protest</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b">Rollback of exam fee increase</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b">Under discussion</td></tr><tr><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">University Fee Hike</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b">Affordable education in IITs/NITs</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b">Partial rollback</td></tr><tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="py-3 px-4 font-medium text-gray-900">Teacher Recruitment Delay</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Filling vacant teaching positions</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Ongoing</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Students Protest</h2>
<p><strong>1. Academic concerns:</strong> Paper leaks, exam irregularities, fee hikes, and delayed results directly affect students' futures.</p>
<p><strong>2. Democratic participation:</strong> Protesting peacefully is a fundamental right under Article 19 of the Indian Constitution.</p>
<p><strong>3. Collective voice:</strong> Individual complaints often go unheard. Collective action brings attention to systemic issues.</p>
<p><strong>4. Social awareness:</strong> Students today are more aware of social issues and want to contribute to change.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Parents Should Respond</h2>
<p><strong>1. Listen first:</strong> Before judging, listen to your child's perspective. Understand why they feel strongly about an issue.</p>
<p><strong>2. Discuss, don't dictate:</strong> Have an open conversation. Share your views but respect their opinion.</p>
<p><strong>3. Safety first:</strong> Ensure your child's safety. Peaceful protest is a right, but violence is never acceptable.</p>
<p><strong>4. Balance activism and academics:</strong> Help your child balance their social involvement with academic responsibilities.</p>
<p><strong>5. Teach critical thinking:</strong> Encourage your child to verify information from multiple sources before forming opinions.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Right Way to Protest</h2>
<p><strong>Peaceful methods:</strong> Marches, petitions, social media campaigns, candlelight vigils, and letter-writing campaigns are all peaceful and effective.</p>
<p><strong>Avoid:</strong> Violence, property damage, blocking traffic, and disrupting exams or classes. These harm the cause and can lead to legal consequences.</p>

<div class="bg-[#035c00]/5 border border-[#035c00]/20 rounded-xl p-5 my-6"><h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Balanced Education</h3><p>JGPS School Joya encourages students to be socially aware while maintaining academic focus. Call <strong>+91 9412137554</strong> for guidance.</p></div>

<div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-5 my-6">
<h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">August 2026 Update</h3>
<p>Student movements in 2026 have led to positive changes — NEET re-exam was conducted, NTA reforms were announced, and exam fee discussions are ongoing. Source: Various news sources (August 2026).</p>
</div>`,
