    authorBio: "Priya specializes in child psychology and mental well-being. She helps students manage exam stress and build a healthy mindset during tough preparation phases.",
    readTime: '4 mins',
    category: "Student Life",
    image: "/images/blog/child-failed-entrance-exam-what-to-do.webp"
    ,
    content: `<p>It is heartbreaking when your child doesn't clear an entrance exam they prepared hard for. But failure is not the end — it is a learning opportunity. Here are 5 practical options for parents when their child fails an entrance exam.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 1: Try Again Next Year</h2>
<p>If your child is eligible for the next year's exam, consider preparing again. Many successful students cleared the exam on their second attempt.</p>
<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Your child knows the exam pattern, has experience, and can improve weak areas.</p>
<p><strong>Cons:</strong> One year gap, emotional stress, and peer pressure.</p>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Children who missed by a small margin and are motivated to try again.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 2: Apply for Other Schools</h2>
<p>If JNVST or AISSEE didn't work out, there are other excellent schools:</p>
<p>• <strong>AMU Schools:</strong> Entrance exam in February, very affordable</p>
<p>• <strong>JMI Schools:</strong> Entrance exam in February, good quality education</p>
<p>• <strong>VidyaGyan:</strong> Free school for rural students</p>
<p>• <strong>EMRS:</strong> Free school for ST students</p>
<p>• <strong>Good private schools:</strong> Many private schools offer scholarships</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 3: Focus on Current School</h2>
<p>If your child is already in a good school, focus on excelling there. Many students from regular schools crack JEE, NEET, and other competitive exams.</p>
<p><strong>Action plan:</strong> Enroll in a good coaching institute, join a study group, and focus on NCERT basics.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 4: Explore Alternative Education</h2>
<p>Traditional school is not the only path. Consider:</p>
<p>• <strong>Homeschooling:</strong> If your child learns better at home</p>
<p>• <strong>Online schools:</strong> Many accredited online schools are available</p>
<p>• <strong>Skill-based courses:</strong> Coding, art, music, or sports academies</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 5: Support Your Child Emotionally</h2>
<p>The most important thing is to support your child emotionally. Failure at a young age can affect self-esteem.</p>
<p><strong>What to say:</strong> "I'm proud of your effort. This exam doesn't define you. Let's figure out the next step together."</p>
<p><strong>What NOT to say:</strong> "You should have studied harder. Sharma ji's son got selected."</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Remember</h2>
<p>Many successful people failed entrance exams. APJ Abdul Kalam failed his first attempt at the Air Force exam. He went on to become the President of India. Failure is not final — it is feedback.</p>

<div class="bg-[#035c00]/5 border border-[#035c00]/20 rounded-xl p-5 my-6"><h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Guidance at JGPS</h3><p>JGPS School Joya provides guidance for students who didn't clear entrance exams. Call <strong>+91 9412137554</strong> for support.</p></div>

<div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-5 my-6">
<h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">August 2026 Update</h3>
<p>If your child didn't clear JNVST or AISSEE 2026, applications for 2027 exams are now open. Start preparing early for better chances. Source: JGPS Academic Team (August 2026).</p>
</div>`,
    faqs:     [{'question': 'What if my child failed JNVST?', 'answer': 'Options include: try again next year, apply for other schools (AMU, JMI, VidyaGyan), focus on current school, or explore alternative education.'}, {'question': 'Should my child try again next year?', 'answer': 'If they missed by a small margin and are motivated, yes. Many students clear on their second attempt.'}, {'question': 'How to support a child who failed?', 'answer': "Be supportive, not critical. Say 'I'm proud of your effort' and help them explore alternatives. Don't compare with other children."}, {'question': 'Are there other good schools besides JNV and Sainik?', 'answer': 'Yes. AMU, JMI, VidyaGyan, EMRS, and good private schools are all excellent options.'}, {'question': 'Is one year gap harmful?', 'answer': 'No. Many students take a gap year for preparation. Use the year productively — strengthen basics, develop skills, and prepare again.'}],
