    content: `<p>CBSE has officially issued guidelines for implementing the three-language policy from the 2026-27 academic session. This is one of the most discussed changes under NEP 2020. Here is what students and parents need to know.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the Three-Language Policy?</h2>
<p>Students must learn <strong>3 languages</strong> until Class 10:</p>
<p><strong>Language 1:</strong> Mother tongue or regional language (mandatory)</p>
<p><strong>Language 2:</strong> English (mandatory)</p>
<p><strong>Language 3:</strong> Hindi or another Indian language (mandatory from Class 6-9)</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CBSE Guidelines for 2026-27</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="min-w-full bg-white border border-gray-200 shadow-sm rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm"><thead class="bg-[#035c00]"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-white text-left">Class</th><th class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold text-white text-left">Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">Class 6-8</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold">3 languages compulsory</td></tr><tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">Class 9</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-bold">3 languages compulsory (new entrants)</td></tr><tr><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b font-medium text-gray-900">Class 10</td><td class="py-3 px-4 border-b">2 languages (existing students exempted from 3rd)</td></tr><tr class="bg-gray-50"><td class="py-3 px-4 font-medium text-gray-900">Class 11-12</td><td class="py-3 px-4">2 languages</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exemptions</h2>
<p><strong>1. Current Class 10 students:</strong> Exempted from the third language requirement. They continue with 2 languages.</p>
<p><strong>2. Students from non-Hindi states:</strong> Can choose any Indian language as the third language, not necessarily Hindi.</p>
<p><strong>3. Foreign students:</strong> Can choose their native language as the third language.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Impact on Students</h2>
<p><strong>1. Additional subject:</strong> Students now have to study one more language, which adds to the academic workload.</p>
<p><strong>2. More teachers needed:</strong> Schools need to hire language teachers, especially for less common languages.</p>
<p><strong>3. Textbook availability:</strong> NCERT is developing textbooks for 22 scheduled languages.</p>
<p><strong>4. Exam pressure:</strong> Third language marks will be included in the final result.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Prepare</h2>
<p><strong>1. Start early:</strong> If your child is in Class 4-5, start introducing the third language now.</p>
<p><strong>2. Use NCERT books:</strong> NCERT is developing textbooks for all 22 scheduled languages.</p>
<p><strong>3. Practice daily:</strong> Spend 30 minutes daily on the third language — reading, writing, and speaking.</p>
<p><strong>4. Use apps:</strong> Language learning apps like Duolingo can help build basic vocabulary.</p>

<div class="bg-[#035c00]/5 border border-[#035c00]/20 rounded-xl p-5 my-6"><h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">NEP-Ready at JGPS</h3><p>JGPS School Joya has implemented the three-language policy with qualified language teachers. Call <strong>+91 9412137554</strong> for admissions.</p></div>

<div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-5 my-6">
<h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">August 2026 Update</h3>
<p>CBSE has issued official guidelines for the three-language policy from 2026-27. Schools are implementing it with a phased approach. Source: CBSE Official (June 2026).</p>
</div>`,
